    "notes": "I changed `extname` in `src/_path.ts:217`, and all 598 tests in the full suite now pass, including both new suites. I didn't change anything outside `src/`.\n\nThe function converts `\\` to `/`, then looks at the part of the path after the last `/`:\n- **Empty, `\".\"` or `\"..\"`:** it returns `\"\"`, so a path ending in a separator still has no extension.\n- **Ends with a dot:** it returns `\".\"`. This covers `\"index.\"`, `\"foo..\"`, `\"...\"` and `\".index.\"`.\n- **Anything else:** it uses the existing regular expression, unchanged.\n\nI didn't touch `parse` or the `posix`/`win32` versions; they call `extname`, so they pick up the new results.\n\nFor the third case I kept the old expression even though the spec's wording of that rule doesn't quite match it. The spec says a dot that is the first character of the last part never starts an extension. But the old expression gives `\".foo\"` for paths like `\"/.foo\"` or `\"a/.foo\"`, where Node gives `\"\"`. The spec also says the existing behaviour must not change, so I kept it, and no test checks this case. Following the wording instead would change what `\"a/.foo\"` returns.\n\nTests left failing: none",
